{"name":"Buckwheat Groats","hex":"#e0d8a7","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buckwheat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Asian plant, of the species Fagopyrum esculentum. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The fruit of this plant used as a pseudocereal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buckwheat"},{"term":"groats","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of groat"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/groats"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buckwheat","blocks":[{"text":"Buckwheat or common buckwheat is a flowering plant in the knotweed family Polygonaceae cultivated for its grain-like seeds and as a cover crop. Buckwheat cultivation originated around the 6th millennium BC in the region of what is now Yunnan Province in southwestern China. The name \"buckwheat\" is used for several other species, such as Fagopyrum tataricum, a domesticated food plant raised in Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Japanese_Buckwheat_Flower.JPG/330px-Japanese_Buckwheat_Flower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buckwheat","wikidata":"Q132734"},{"title":"Groat (grain)","blocks":[{"text":"Groats are the hulled kernels of various cereal grains, such as oats, wheat, rye, and barley. Groats are whole grains that include the cereal germ and fiber-rich bran portion of the grain, as well as the endosperm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Pigeons_food_cereals_mix.jpg/330px-Pigeons_food_cereals_mix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groat_(grain)","wikidata":"Q1436715"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Fagopyrum_esculentum1.jpg/250px-Fagopyrum_esculentum1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wi